Z||000005||Oreif||11-09-2000||10:47 AM||||Nice looking car. Yes it needs lowering springs. (I think all fieros need to be lowered  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ) As for the radio sticking out, a dremel tool will remove the back piece and you can get a different adapter to sink it even with the console face. Tell me more about the woofer in the console! What size woofer? How exactly did you mount it? Does the glovebox open to show how it is installed?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000006||1FST2M6||11-09-2000||10:49 AM||||i just used a flat head screw driver.. and stuck it under the plate and turned it... worked on the SE and the GT!||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||11-09-2000||11:13 AM||||Friends,  <P>I guess I was the dumb one... again.  I tried to go after the rivets with a hammer and chisel.  Seemed easy enough.  I tried my Dremel, but didn't think of using a cut-off wheel.  Long story short, it took damn near 2 hours the first time I did it... you know what the problem was don't you?  I couldn't swing the BFH inside the car.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000008||RiceCooker||11-09-2000||01:11 PM||||Thanks, guys!<BR>Special thanks to 1FST2M6 for posting these pics.<BR>- I definitley agree that I need to lower it. It's first on my list of things to do in the spring (no pun intended)<BR>- Exhaust system: Fiero store sprint manifolds, 2 1/4" piping all the way back, no cat, flowmaster muffler, Monza dual exhaust tips (no reasonators).I like the factory tips - the Monza ones are chrome and a little bit bigger and they're not ricy. This car is loud! People tell me it sounds like a V8 (it better, cause it cost me an arm and a leg) <BR>- Those graphics, believe it or not, are factory. I think they were a dealer option, but they were on the car when my Aunt bought it new in Sherwood Park, Alberta. I've never seen them on another Fiero. They are 15 years old though, so they are starting to peel in some spots.<BR>- Under the stereo is my Alpine security display. My alarm has all the toys - shock sensor, proximity, etc. You can adjust each of these to 30 levels of sensitivity for pre-warn and alarm. You can find out if a sensor is out or what set your car off, etc. You can even check the battery!<BR>- My stereo sticks out just because of the adapter kit used to install it. I'm definitely going to fix it.<BR>- That's a 51/4" speaker with a tweeter (mounted up front) I built a box in the console and mounted the speaker to that with a piece of plexiglass on top so you can see down to the speaker and still put a thing or two in the glove box. The speaker grill isn't attached to the speaker, I just cut the glove box and basically, stuck the grill in. The speaker is hooked up to my Amp as a center channel - I just bridged left and right so it's not a true center channel, just mono.<BR>- If 1FST2M6 wants to post the other pics, you can see my disc changer, and my 6x9's in the door.<P>- I put it away this week for the winter and I'm taking the bus/train. I sure will appreciate my car even more in the spring.<P>Thanks trav<P>Later, <BR>Biff||||reg||6
